				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=732274&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930883" alt="thedeadlymoose" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=732274)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;" >thedeadlymoose</a></span> <span class="odate time_1329717537 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Feb 2012 05:58</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1377931">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I really like the idea that The Foundation is both the creator and containment group for all of these things, with one side not knowing a thing about the other. The last paragraph kind of feels like a cop out.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's always possible that the Foundation is <em>still</em> the creator, but they've set in motion a creation process which they can never really hope to understand.</p>
<p>Or the narrator is lying, hoping to absolve themselves of some responsibility in the eyes of the presumed reader, because they're ashamed.</p>
<p>I like all of these statements plus the more obvious one (that the narrator is telling the truth - they aren't really in control, not truly, and <em>they never were</em> - which is also my favorite because it's the biggest <em>oh shit</em> moment from where I'm standing, taking all the SCPs, even the bland or fun ones, and putting a huge vast dark unknown <em>something</em> behind them that us humans have not or cannot directly conceived of <em>and it's getting eager</em>&#8230; you get my point). All hinge on that paragraph existing. Which is why I thoroughly like it.</p>

							</div>

								
